                <h2>Why Tier Account Management Instead of Intuitive Allocation?</h2>
<p>More accounts don't necessarily mean better results. Many teams open ten accounts casually at the start, only to discover budget dispersion, data silos, and even internal competition for the same audience later on.</p>
<p>Account tiering is fundamentally about <strong>resource classification</strong>—allocating limited budgets, attention, and risk exposure to accounts of different value levels. Once tiering is clear, decisions become straightforward: which accounts test new creatives, which to protect during market volatility.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Are the Main Account Tiering Models?</h2>
<p>Three models dominate in practice:</p>
<p><strong>1. Lifecycle-Based Tiering</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Incubator Accounts</strong>: New accounts or creative testing phases with low budgets ($100-500 daily), higher CPA variance accepted</li>
<li><strong>Core Accounts</strong>: Mature accounts with validated ROI models, stable budgets, focused on creative iteration</li>
<li><strong>Long-Tail Accounts</strong>: Consistently running but limited volume, low maintenance cost, typically covering niche audiences</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Platform-Based Tiering</strong></p>
<p>Meta, TikTok, and Google each have different account structures and policies. Grouping accounts by platform streamlines reconciliation and top-up management.</p>
<p><strong>3. Business Objective-Based Tiering</strong></p>
<p>Separating "brand awareness accounts" from "direct response accounts"—the former measures CPM and reach, the latter tracks ROAS and CPA. Mixing two metric systems leads to confused decision-making.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How to Allocate Budget Across Tiers?</h2>
<p>A practical reference ratio (adjustable based on your business):</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Account Tier</th>
<th>Recommended Share</th>
<th>Adjustment Frequency</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Core Accounts</td>
<td>60-70%</td>
<td>Weekly review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Incubator Accounts</td>
<td>20-30%</td>
<td>Daily or every other day</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Long-Tail Accounts</td>
<td>5-10%</td>
<td>Monthly check</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Key Principle</strong>: Don't invest core budgets into incubator accounts. One utility app team poured 80% of their budget into three new accounts testing new creatives—two weeks later, they had zero data to show for it and had to start over completely.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How to Manage Top-Ups and Balance Alerts Without Pitfalls?</h2>
<p>Under multi-account management, two common top-up issues emerge:</p>
<p><strong>Problem 1: Scattered Top-Ups Increase Administrative Burden</strong></p>
<p>Establish a "centralized top-up + automatic distribution" mechanism. For instance, platforms like YiyuMedia support consolidated balance management across accounts, with automatic transfer rules to eliminate the need for manual logins per account.</p>
<p><strong>Problem 2: Sudden Traffic Spikes Drain Account Balances</strong></p>
<p>Set balance alert thresholds: Core accounts trigger warnings when balance falls below 2x daily budget; long-tail accounts alert at $500. Notifications should fire immediately to prevent campaigns running with zero balance.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How to Implement Risk Control Isolation?</h2>
<p>The real fear isn't one account being suspended—it's one account's violation dragging down others through shared降权.</p>
<p><strong>Three Isolation Principles:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Domain/Website Isolation</strong>: Landing pages for different accounts should use independent domains or subdomains where possible to reduce association risk</li>
<li><strong>Payment Method Isolation</strong>: Don't绑 the same credit card to too many accounts—payment method association is a major platform identification factor</li>
<li><strong>Creative Isolation</strong>: Don't deploy winning creatives across all accounts simultaneously; validate performance first, then scale gradually to reduce single-point risk</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>How to Assign Permissions in Multi-Person Operations?</h2>
<p>The biggest collaboration problem in multi-account management: one person changes creatives while another adjusts budgets, then account performance crashes and no one knows who to blame.</p>
<p><strong>Recommend Role-Based Permission Assignment:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Media Buyers</strong>: Full account operation permissions, responsible for creative updates and bid adjustments</li>
<li><strong>Finance/Ops</strong>: Read-only access, can view spending data but cannot directly operate</li>
<li><strong>Administrators</strong>: Audit large top-ups, new account authorization, account structure changes</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>How to Efficiently Reconcile Multi-Account Finances?</h2>
<p>End-of-month reconciliation is often the most painful process. Several efficiency-boosting practices:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unified Statement Format</strong>: Export all platform statements and consolidate using a standardized template to prevent omissions during cross-platform reconciliation</li>
<li><strong>Tier-Based Report Generation</strong>: Generate detailed reports for core accounts separately; aggregate spending data for incubator accounts is sufficient</li>
<li><strong>Reconciliation Cadence</strong>: Reconcile core accounts weekly, aggregate long-tail accounts monthly to reduce unnecessary labor</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Final Assessment</h2>
<p>Multi-account tiering management isn't a complex engineering project—the core lies in three things: <strong>clear tiering, centralized budgeting, and isolated risk control</strong>. Once tiering standards are established, top-up timing stabilizes; when top-ups aren't chaotic, financial reconciliation becomes effortless; when risk control is solid, core accounts can keep running sustainably.</p>
<p>Sort your existing accounts into core/incubator/long-tail categories first—it's more important than rushing to open new accounts.</p>
